You might call it a bucket of water even if it is empty if it is used
for water, but that's totally beside the point. We are not talking
about the bucket itself, but about the water, quantified in "buckets".
If you knew Esperanto, I could tell you that it is the difference
between "sitelo de akvo" = "a bucket of (characterized by) water"
and "sitelo da akvo" = "water in quantity 'a bucket of', a bucketful
of water".

Anyway, I would never say that a bucket of water has exactly two
hydrogen and one oxygen atom, under any interpretation of "a bucket
of water".

